INTRODUCTORY NOTE TO RULE 11 AND BROWN V. AMERIPRISE FINANCIAL
Put yourself in the role of a lawyer who represents plaintiffs on a contingency fee basis and who is often approached by potential clients—people who have been injured in an accident, fired from their job, or charged for something they don’t remember purchasing. You interview the individuals and hear their stories. You discover that some of the injuries are small—so small that they’ll cost more to pursue than they could hope to recover. You discover that some of the grievances don’t have a legal basis for recovery; whether just or unjust, the law provides no avenue for relief. For these individuals, there is nothing to gain from litigation.

You also identify some grievances that have a legal basis for recovery and involve damages large enough that litigation is worthwhile. For some of these, the individuals’ account of what happened provides a compelling basis for demanding legal relief. For others, you conduct additional investigation and determine that there is indeed a plausible claim for relief. In such cases, you will take on the individuals as clients, draft a detailed complaint, and easily head off a motion to dismiss. What about the remainder, the claims with potentially high stakes but where the individual’s story doesn’t support a claim for legal redress? What do you do with those?

Most lawyers who represent plaintiffs will tell you that one of the most important parts of their job is turning away potential clients. It saves the lawyer from spending time and money on a fruitless cause, and more importantly it protects their would-be client from the anxiety and hassle of litigation when it is likely to end in disappointment. Putting it more coarsely, lawyer and statesman Elihu Root quipped, “About half the practice of a decent lawyer is telling would-be clients that they are damned fools and should shut up.”

But instead of turning away a plaintiff with a weak but big-ticket claim, why not just invent favorable facts and plead those? After all, most cases settle
