early on, and if the demand is large enough, even a settlement for pennies on the dollar could be lucrative for both lawyer and client. Or what if, in the case where the lawyer interviews a potential client and determines that more investigation would be appropriate, the lawyer doesn’t want to bother with the time and expense of further investigation? Why not save some time, skip the investigation, and just guess the facts when drafting the complaint?

Why not? It is unethical. It violates rules of professional conduct. It violates federal statutes and court rules prohibiting groundless filings, only one of which is Rule 11, which we focus on here. And it casts a pall of doubt and suspicion upon legitimate claims brought by other lawyers and plaintiffs.

But it happens.

Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 11
Signing Pleadings, Motions, and Other Papers;
Representations to the Court; Sanctions

* * *

(b) Representations to the Court. By presenting to the court a pleading, written motion, or other paper—whether by signing, filing, submitting, or later advocating it—an attorney or unrepresented party certifies that to the best of the person’s knowledge, information, and belief, formed after an inquiry reasonable under the circumstances:

(1) it is not being presented for any improper purpose, such as to harass, cause unnecessary delay, or needlessly increase the cost of litigation;

(2) the claims, defenses, and other legal contentions are warranted by existing law or by a nonfrivolous argument for extending, modifying, or reversing existing law or for establishing new law;

(3) the factual contentions have evidentiary support or, if specifically so identified, will likely have evidentiary support after a reasonable opportunity for further investigation or discovery; and

(4) the denials of factual contentions are warranted on the evidence or, if specifically so identified, are reasonably based on belief or a lack of information.

